WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Reports turn the plan into a focused explanation for a
person or a decision.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:14.545
This lesson covers eight report types, shared scope
controls, coordinator presets, explicit progress
observations and useful output.

00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:22.965
We will distinguish current status, forecast, workload,
critical path, milestones, baseline changes and historical
progress.

00:00:23.085 --> 00:00:29.260
The same dataset can answer different questions, so choose
the question before formatting the result.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.705
Nothing in this lesson automatically sends a message to
your team.

00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:41.733
Task check-in prepares a focused follow-up for each
selected person.

00:00:41.853 --> 00:00:47.553
Choose the reporting date and reply-by date, then inspect
the assigned tasks and questions.

00:00:47.673 --> 00:00:56.998
Ongoing includes unfinished work that has started, is past
planned finish or has positive progress, with unresolved
dates controlled separately.

00:00:57.118 --> 00:01:02.168
Check who will receive which tasks, especially for shared
or unassigned work.

00:01:02.288 --> 00:01:08.063
The app prepares text for you to copy; it does not send
email or a message automatically.

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:17.992
Resource workload and assignment coverage answers who is
carrying the work and what is not assigned clearly.

00:01:18.112 --> 00:01:27.037
Inspect allocation, capacity and any unresolved scheduling
information before using a summary as evidence that the
team is feasible.

00:01:27.157 --> 00:01:33.382
An unassigned task can disappear from a person-only view
while remaining important to delivery.

00:01:33.502 --> 00:01:38.102
Compare the report with the resource panel when
investigating a conflict.

00:01:38.222 --> 00:01:45.397
A useful coordinator summary includes that missing
ownership rather than making the plan look tidier by
omission.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:56.575
The Progress forecast report uses the same calculations
and saved remaining-work assumptions as the forecast view.

00:01:56.695 --> 00:02:03.645
Its as-of date begins with the saved forecast date or
today, and changing it produces a report scenario.

00:02:03.765 --> 00:02:07.565
Explain which date and assumptions the report uses.

00:02:07.685 --> 00:02:13.235
A projected finish is not an observation that the source
has already been rescheduled.

00:02:13.355 --> 00:02:19.430
Compare planned and forecast work, then trace a relevant
cause before sharing a conclusion.

00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:31.183
Critical path and schedule sensitivity helps identify work
with little or no scheduling flexibility in the modeled
network.

00:02:31.303 --> 00:02:35.778
Inspect the dependency chain and close to critical
threshold.

00:02:35.898 --> 00:02:42.498
Near-critical work deserves attention even when it is not
the single most restrictive path at this moment.

00:02:42.618 --> 00:02:50.718
This report depends on the modeled schedule and its
assumptions; missing dependencies can produce a
misleadingly comfortable result.

00:02:50.838 --> 00:02:58.788
Explain the relevant chain and the threshold you chose
rather than treating a highlighted task as an unexplained
risk label.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:07.425
Milestone and delivery outlook focuses the conversation on
delivery events.

00:03:07.545 --> 00:03:14.895
Trace a milestone back to the work that drives it and
identify the status or forecast facts behind any concern.

00:03:15.015 --> 00:03:21.715
A milestone marker is not a block of effort, and a
highlighted deadline is not proof that the date is
achievable.

00:03:21.835 --> 00:03:32.235
Use the report to make a specific conversation possible:
which delivery event is affected, which work matters, and
what decision or clarification is needed next.

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:40.892
Progress and due-date outlook summarizes completion and
due-date status.

00:03:41.012 --> 00:03:48.237
Read the as-of date, task scope and treatment of
unresolved schedules before interpreting totals or charts.

00:03:48.357 --> 00:03:55.057
A percentage may summarize reported work without
explaining whether the remaining effort is realistic.

00:03:55.177 --> 00:03:59.077
Compare the detailed tasks when an aggregate looks
surprising.

00:03:59.197 --> 00:04:08.547
Use notes or a follow-up to clarify uncertainty rather
than hiding it behind a compact report that appears more
precise than the plan actually is.

00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:20.217
Historical burndown and burnup requires at least two
explicit observations and stable aliases for every task
and milestone.

00:04:20.337 --> 00:04:23.562
Previewing a report does not create history.

00:04:23.682 --> 00:04:30.832
Record a snapshot for the ninth of November, update the
reported progress, then record another for the sixteenth.

00:04:30.952 --> 00:04:36.927
Select the historical report and compare fixed baseline
scope with current scope over time.

00:04:37.047 --> 00:04:40.897
Explain whether scope changes belong in the comparison.

00:04:41.017 --> 00:04:47.742
Save the document to retain these pinned observations in
the portable file; they are separate from undo history.

00:04:51.267 --> 00:04:56.042
Changes since baseline compares the current plan with a
retained reference.

00:04:56.162 --> 00:05:00.062
Save a baseline before making the change you want to
discuss.

00:05:00.182 --> 00:05:05.082
We will then update Backend's completion and inspect the
baseline report.

00:05:05.202 --> 00:05:14.777
A useful review identifies what changed and why, rather
than presenting every task as newly added because the
reference was not the intended plan.

00:05:14.897 --> 00:05:23.947
Keep this report separate from a historical progress
chart: a baseline comparison and a sequence of dated
observations answer different questions.

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:34.342
Coordinator summary and Delivery outlook presets begin
with broader scope than a person-specific check-in.

00:05:34.462 --> 00:05:40.637
Compare the two presets and inspect which tasks,
milestones and unassigned work they include.

00:05:40.757 --> 00:05:45.682
A preset is a starting configuration, not a reason to skip
review.

00:05:45.802 --> 00:05:49.827
Canvas filters do not silently define the report scope.

00:05:49.947 --> 00:05:58.422
Check dates and recipients again after switching report
type, especially when the result will be used to
coordinate work across several people.

00:06:01.933 --> 00:06:07.108
Content and layout controls decide how much supporting
material to include.

00:06:07.228 --> 00:06:14.353
Notes, task links, unresolved schedules, milestones,
compact output and charts serve different audiences.

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:19.623
Introduction and sign-off fields help the prepared message
fit your context.

00:06:19.743 --> 00:06:24.993
Keep the underlying facts and uncertainty visible while
choosing the presentation.

00:06:25.113 --> 00:06:31.013
A compact result should not imply that unassigned or
unresolved work has ceased to exist.

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:36.308
Inspect the complete preview before copying a message or
downloading a report.

00:06:39.833 --> 00:06:44.958
Filter the canvas to Alice and note that this is a view
choice, not a deletion.

00:06:45.078 --> 00:06:50.903
Open Reports and explicitly choose all tasks for the
report as of November sixteenth.

00:06:51.023 --> 00:06:56.598
Select Sam as the preview recipient and set a reply date
of November eighteenth.

00:06:56.718 --> 00:07:01.868
Backend remains in Sam’s report even though the canvas is
filtered to Alice.

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:06.888
Change the compact layout and check the same task and
recipient facts again.

00:07:07.008 --> 00:07:14.358
The report controls determine its scope; they do not
silently inherit the canvas filter or change the authored
plan.

00:07:17.867 --> 00:07:20.592
Reports capture a view of the plan.

00:07:20.712 --> 00:07:27.687
If the source changes while a report is open, the app
warns that the plan changed and offers Refresh report.

00:07:27.807 --> 00:07:31.907
Do not share a stale preview as though it were a current
calculation.

00:07:32.027 --> 00:07:41.302
Close the report, change a progress value, reopen and
inspect the result; when working concurrently, look for
the stale warning and refresh explicitly.

00:07:41.422 --> 00:07:47.972
The useful habit is to check the report's date, scope and
freshness immediately before using its output.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:55.925
Keep a report open while another editor changes the shared
plan.

00:07:56.045 --> 00:08:00.945
The second browser updates Backend progress from ten to
forty percent.

00:08:01.065 --> 00:08:07.665
The open report now warns that the plan changed, and its
output controls are disabled until you refresh.

00:08:07.785 --> 00:08:14.910
Read that warning and choose Refresh report, then inspect
the updated report before copying or downloading it.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.855
This prevents an older captured report from being mistaken
for the current plan.

00:08:19.975 --> 00:08:24.800
The change is real collaboration traffic between the two
training browsers.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:34.150
Use the supported output controls to copy or download the
prepared result after reviewing it.

00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:42.995
Check a pasted or exported example rather than assuming
that rich text, charts and plain text will look identical
in every destination.

00:08:43.115 --> 00:08:47.815
Keep links and recipient-specific content appropriate for
the audience.

00:08:47.935 --> 00:08:54.485
The app does not send these check-ins, so the final choice
of recipient and delivery channel remains with you.

00:08:54.605 --> 00:09:00.255
Save relevant progress observations separately when they
should remain part of the document.

00:09:03.767 --> 00:09:10.492
Pause and prepare a check-in for each assigned person,
then a coordinator view that includes unassigned work.

00:09:10.612 --> 00:09:17.037
Explain a critical or near-critical chain, inspect a
milestone and compare progress with forecast.

00:09:17.157 --> 00:09:22.557
Record two explicit observations and explain the scope of
the historical chart.

00:09:22.677 --> 00:09:28.502
Finally, save a baseline, make one clear change and
identify it in the baseline report.

00:09:28.622 --> 00:09:35.747
You have succeeded when each result answers a distinct
question and its date, scope and assumptions are
understandable.

00:09:39.267 --> 00:09:44.667
Choose the report for the question, then check dates,
scope, assumptions and freshness.

00:09:44.787 --> 00:09:50.862
Keep current status, forecast, baseline changes and
historical observations distinct.

00:09:50.982 --> 00:09:57.357
Review the output before copying or exporting, and
remember that preparing a message does not send it.

00:09:57.477 --> 00:10:07.327
The following lessons return to scope structure and
connected diagrams, so the work behind these reports
remains organized and traceable as the project evolves.
